# {{ project_name }}

A Frust app created with `frust create`.

## Structure

{%- if arch == "clean-signals" %}
- `src/lib.rs` — the entry point (`frust::app!`) and the root component.
- `src/failure.rs` — the app's error type, shared across layers.
- `src/features/counter/` — the counter feature slice with a `clean-signals`-driven
  component lifecycle (`ControllerCore`, `CounterController`):
  - `domain/` — the business logic layer (`CounterRepository` trait, use cases).
  - `data/` — the data layer (repository impl, data sources).
  - `presentation/` — the UI layer (`controllers.rs`, `pages.rs` with `counter_page()`).

The architecture layers all your business logic into the feature slice; `lib.rs`
stays shallow and only wires the app's `app!()` entry and component's `State`.
{%- else %}
- `src/lib.rs` — the entry point (`frust::app!`) and the root component.
- `src/home_page.rs` — the counter screen, a `Component` built from small widget fns.

Add screens as components in their own modules, and keep `build` shallow by
pulling stateless pieces out into small widget fns.
{%- endif %}

## Theming

This app uses the **Material 3 design system** by default — the
`frust-material` plugin crate dependency in `Cargo.toml`, installed by the
`frust_material::install()` call in `src/lib.rs`'s `frust::app!(setup = {..})`
block — which adapts to the device's light/dark preference.
To swap to Glyph or Cupertino, replace the `frust-material` dependency with
`frust-glyph`/`frust-cupertino` and swap the setup call:

```rust
frust::app!(MyAppApp, setup = {
    // Material 3 (default)
    frust_material::install();

    // Or Glyph
    // frust_glyph::install();

    // Or Cupertino
    // frust_cupertino::install();
});
```

Each `install()` is shorthand for
`frust::set_default_theme(frust_material::baseline())` (and likewise for the
others) — call `set_default_theme` directly instead if you want the tokens
without the crate's install-timing contract.

## Custom Fonts

`frust-material`'s `install()` registers two bundled faces, Roboto Flex and
Roboto Mono, so Material typography resolves out of the box (disable the
crate's default `bundled-fonts` feature to compile them out). `frust-glyph`
likewise bundles and registers Space Mono and IBM Plex Mono; `frust-cupertino`
ships no typefaces and uses the platform's system faces. Register your own fonts
at app startup regardless of design system — `register_app_fonts` takes the
raw bytes of one TTF/OTF (or TTC/OTC) file per call. Call it in the same
`setup = { .. }` block as `install()`:

```rust
frust::app!(MyAppApp, setup = {
    frust_material::install();
    frust::register_app_fonts(include_bytes!("../assets/MyFont.ttf").to_vec());
});
```

## Theme Customization

Customize colors, motion, and spacing by building on a baseline theme with
`Theme::builder(base)`, then force it with `set_app_theme` (call it from the
`setup` block, or later to switch themes at runtime):

```rust
use frust::{Color, Theme, set_app_theme};

fn customize() {
    let custom = Theme::builder(frust_material::baseline())
        .map_colors_light(|mut scheme| {
            scheme.primary = Color::from_rgb8(0x1B, 0x6E, 0xF3);
            scheme
        })
        .build();
    set_app_theme(custom);
}
```

`set_app_theme` pins the theme end-to-end (it no longer follows the system
light/dark preference); `frust::clear_app_theme()` returns to the default.
See `frust-theme`'s docs in the Frust repo for the full token reference.
